===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|treden}}, from {{inh|en|ang|tredan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*tredan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*trudaną}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|treads|treading|trod|past2=tread|past3=treaded|trodden|past_ptc2=trod|past_ptc3=tread|past_ptc4=treaded}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[step]] or [[walk]] (on or across something); to [[trample]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''trod''' back and forth wearily.}}
#: {{ux|en|Don't '''tread''' on the lawn.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Joshua|14|9|passage=And Moses sware on that day, saying, Surely the land whereon thy feet haue '''troden''', shall be thine inheritance, and thy childrens for euer, because thou hast wholly followed the Lord my God.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1711|author=w:Alexander Pope|title=w:An Essay on Criticism|others=part III|passage=Fools rush in where angels fear to '''tread'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1667|author=w:John Milton|title=Paradise Lost|passage=yee that walk The Earth, and stately '''tread''', or lowly creep}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[step]] or [[walk]] upon.
#: {{ux|en|Actors '''tread''' the boards.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively|with certain [[adverb]]s of [[manner]]}} To [[proceed]], to [[behave]] (in a certain manner).
#: {{uxi|en|to '''tread''' lightly, to '''tread''' gently}}
#: {{uxi|en|to '''tread''' carefully, to '''tread''' cautiously, to '''tread''' warily}}
# To beat or press with the feet.
#: {{ux|en|to '''tread''' a path; to '''tread''' land when too light; a well-'''trodden''' path}}
# To work a [[lever]], [[treadle]], etc., with the foot or the feet.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=251|passage=Round about them was a circle of girls and wives of the neighbouring tenants; "they '''trod''' the spinning-wheels with diligent feet, or were using the scraping carding-combs," as an author has it.}}
# To go through or accomplish by walking, dancing, etc.
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|The Knight of Malta|5|2|passage=I am resolved to forsake Malta, '''tread''' a pilgrimage to fair Jerusalem.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Love's Labour's Lost|V|ii|passage=They have measured many a mile, / To '''tread''' a measure with you on this grass.}}
# {{senseid|en|repress}} To [[crush]] under the foot; to [[trample]] in contempt or hatred; to [[subdue]]; to [[repress]].
#: {{syn|en|step on}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|book=Psalms|chapter=44|verse=5|passage=Through thee will we push down our enemies: through thy name will we '''tread''' them under that rise up against us.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[copulate]]; said of (especially [[male]]) [[bird]]s.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Love's Labour's Lost|5|2|passage=When Turtles '''tread''', and Rookes and Dawes,<br>And Maidens bleach their summer smockes:}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|of a male bird}} To copulate with.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1927|author=Havelock Ellis|title=Studies in the Psychology of Sex, Volume 2 (of 6)|chapter=|edition=|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/13611/13611-h/13611-h.htm|passage=Thus, a poultry-breeder describes a hen (colored Dorking) crowing like a cock, only somewhat more harshly, as a cockerel crows, and with an enormous comb, larger than is ever seen in the male. This bird used to try to '''tread''' her fellow-hens.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[crush]] [[grapes]] with one's feet to make [[wine]]
#: {{syn|en|[[stomp]]}}

=====Usage notes=====
* {{m|en||Treaded}} is not commonly used in the UK and is less common in the US as well. It is apparently used more often in {{m|en|tread water}}.
* {{m|en||Tread}} is sometimes used as a past and past participle, especially in the US.

